**Summary:** If all you need is a basic desktop meeting recorder that only works on Windows and recording quality isn’t a priority, using Window’s native capture APIs to build one makes sense. You can build a working prototype with WASAPI and Windows Graphics Capture fairly quickly. But as you scale to hundreds of recording hours or expand to other operating systems like macOS and Linux, you’ll need to build and support hundreds of different integrations. Recall.ai’s Desktop Recording SDK provides the reliability and scalability needed to support high-volume recording.


In this blog, I’ll walk through how I used Windows capture APIs like WASAPI and Windows Graphics Capture to build a desktop meeting recorder. I’ll share[my code](https://github.com/recallai/windows-desktop-app) and working demo, along with the limitations I encountered working with Window’s capture APIs.


## Using WASAPI and Windows Graphic Capture to build a desktop meeting recorder


Windows offers several native APIs for audio and video capture. To build a desktop meeting recorder, I used Windows Graphics Capture and Windows Audio Session API (WASAPI). While Windows offers several native APIs for audio and video capture, Windows Graphics Capture is the only Windows recording API that supports recording individual windows instead of the entire display and WASAPI is the only native Windows API that captures system audio not just microphone audio without requiring a dedicated loopback device.


WASAPI captures both microphone and system audio on Windows separately using standard capture and loopback capture. Standard capture records the microphone input, while loopback capture records meeting audio playing through the speakers or headphones. The Windows API for audio doesn’t require custom audio routing or additional drivers to capture audio. However, WASAPI doesn’t automatically handle mute-state detection or audio device changes, both of which are needed for privacy reasons.


```text
// Sample showcasing how to initialize WASAPI


// Assumes COM is initialized.
// Error handling and cleanup are omitted for brevity.
IMMDeviceEnumerator* enumerator = nullptr;
IMMDevice* endpoint = nullptr;
IAudioClient* audioClient = nullptr;
IAudioCaptureClient* captureClient = nullptr;
WAVEFORMATEX* format = nullptr;


CoCreateInstance(
__uuidof(MMDeviceEnumerator), nullptr, CLSCTX_ALL,
IID_PPV_ARGS(&enumerator));


// Select the default speakers/headset for system audio.
enumerator->GetDefaultAudioEndpoint(
eRender, eConsole, &endpoint);


endpoint->Activate(
__uuidof(IAudioClient), CLSCTX_ALL,
nullptr, reinterpret_cast<void**>(&audioClient));


audioClient->GetMixFormat(&format);


// Capture the output device's audio using shared loopback mode.
audioClient->Initialize(
AUDCLNT_SHAREMODE_SHARED,
AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_LOOPBACK,
1000000,  // Requested buffer duration: 100 ms
0, format, nullptr);


audioClient->GetService(IID_PPV_ARGS(&captureClient));
audioClient->Start();


```


I used Windows Graphics Capture to record video frames from the meeting window, including participant video and shared screens. However, Windows Graphics Capture captures the entire application window, including UI elements such as browser tabs, address bars, and meeting controls. Windows Graphics Capture cannot automatically identify which areas contain meeting content. You need additional logic to identify the boundaries of participant video and shared screens, crop out unwanted UI elements, and update those boundaries as the meeting layout changes. When participants join or leave, or someone shares their screen, the meeting display gets rearranged. Because Windows Graphics Capture doesn't identify these changes automatically, the recorder must detect them and determine the new capture boundaries to avoid cutting off meeting content or including unwanted UI elements.


```text
// Sample showcasing how to initialize Windows Graphics Capture


using namespace winrt::Windows::Graphics::Capture;
using namespace winrt::Windows::Graphics::DirectX;


// Create two buffers for incoming video frames.
auto framePool = Direct3D11CaptureFramePool::CreateFreeThreaded(
device,
DirectXPixelFormat::B8G8R8A8UIntNormalized,
2,
item.Size());


// Receive frames on the frame pool's worker thread.
framePool.FrameArrived([](auto const& pool, auto const&) {
auto frame = pool.TryGetNextFrame();
if (!frame) return;


auto surface = frame.Surface();
auto timestamp = frame.SystemRelativeTime();
auto size = frame.ContentSize();


// Copy the surface into an encoder-owned texture here.
// Use the timestamp to align video with captured audio.


frame.Close(); // Return the capture buffer to the pool.
});


auto session = framePool.CreateCaptureSession(item);
session.StartCapture();


```


