Building a basic desktop meeting recorder on Windows was a fairly straightforward experience especially compared to building one on macOS. While Apple has[native capture APIs](https://www.recall.ai/blog/macos-screencapture-api) like[Core Audio Taps,](https://www.recall.ai/blog/core-audio-taps)[AVFoundation,](https://www.recall.ai/blog/av-foundation) and[ScreenCaptureKit](https://www.recall.ai/blog/how-to-use-screencapturekit-to-record-a-meeting) that can be used[to build a desktop meeting recorder,](https://www.recall.ai/blog/how-to-build-a-desktop-recording-app) working with macOS’ native APIs involves more complexity around permissions, OS version compatibility, and setup.


Both Windows and macOS’ native recording APIs provide the building blocks for a basic meeting recorder. But they lack features like automatic meeting and mute detection, which are a few of the many end-user facing capabilities needed to build a reliable desktop meeting recorder.


## What desktop capture APIs like WASAPI and Windows Graphic Capture don’t cover


To create a robust and reliable desktop meeting recorder, developers must build a long list of capabilities that are not provided by any meeting recording APIs. Otherwise, both user adoption and a desktop recorder’s utility become limited.


Automatic meeting detection recognizes when a user joins a meeting on platforms like Google Meet, Zoom, or Microsoft Teams and starts recording automatically. Without automatic meeting detection, users would need to remember to record meetings. Automatic meeting detection reduces the percentage of meetings that go unrecorded from 88% to just 2%.


Then there are features designed for user privacy, such as in-app mute detection and browser tab isolation. Mute detection ensures that when a user mutes themselves during a meeting and says something, no speech is captured. Browser tab isolation only records the meeting tab, even if the user switches to another tab during the meeting. Mute detection and tab isolation reduce the risk of sensitive information getting accidentally captured and neither are built into WASAPI or other native Windows APIs.


In addition to protecting user privacy, a robust desktop meeting recorder also needs to capture audio and video reliably across different device configurations and user behavior. For example, if an end-user switches microphones or headphones in the middle of a meeting, the recorder should detect the change and continue capturing audio without interruption. If an end-user’s device is running low on battery or available memory, the recorder should minimize its resource consumption like the Desktop Recording SDK does and continue recording without causing additional strain. While Windows' native capture APIs provide the underlying functionality to capture meeting audio and video separately, they do not have features around user privacy and device resource consumption built in.


Recall.ai's Desktop Recording SDK handles the gaps left by Windows capture APIs that impact user adoption while also capturing separate audio and video streams. Because separate audio streams give each meeting participant its own audio stream, audio using the Desktop Recording SDK is by default diarized and speaker-labeled transcripts are generated. Downstream AI applications need structured context around the conversation instead of just a wall of text. Recall.ai provides diarized audio recordings and speaker-labeled transcripts, rather than building and maintaining their own recording infrastructure.


